Ok, I don't know how much you want to avoid spoilers so I will keep this as
plain as possible. I'm going to start with the inevitable read the guide
book, it has tons of information and will be very helpful. When you post
questions you should state what level you want to be spoiled so as not to
spoil your enjoyment of the game.


> 1. How do I know how much damage a weapon does. For example in Diablo
> each weapon's damage number is indicated. In NH, I often don't know
> which weapon to wield, because I don't know which one's better. Should
> I wield my axe, or this sword, or perhaps this club ...which one will
> do more damage?
Well there is a game that Nethack is not, but is based on. The old AD&D
game. If you have passing familarity with that then you kind of know what
some of the better weapons are. This is not an exact crossover. But in
general the bigger a weapon is the more damage it does.


> 2. What are statues, sinks, altars and wells/fountains (good) for? OK,
> I know a few things about them, for example, that one can #dip random
> things into a fountain and see what happens, and that one can find
> items under statues. But how? I tried (s)earch while standing on a
> statue, but nothing ever happens?
> What about those other things (altars, thrones, sinks,
> engravement/grafitti), what are they good for?
Well there are two non spoiler ways you can figure this out. First way is to
go to the help file and try each command on each dungeon feature. The second
way is a little more fun. Most objects it follows a kind of logic what can
be done. For example what can you do at a sink or drinking fountain.

As for Alters, that should be covered in the guidebook, but they do a number
of things, mainly with the #pray and #offer command. With thrones think of
what you can do, grafitti gives hints weather true or not is up to you to
determine.

Something you should do is start a new character and try all of the commands
out. This will help quite a bit.

> 3. How do the different races affect the game(play)? I have never
> noticed any difference, allthough I have played them all at least a
> few times. I only noticed, that orcs need much more food!?
Different races have different "intrinsics". Intrinsics are abilities your
character can use naturaly and starting out they reflect what a race can do.
For example dwarves live underground so they get dark vision. Your race also
effects your alingment choices and what makes you a cannible.

> 4. I think I am stuck on Dlvl 6. There are two small rooms connected
> by a corridor. I have searched at least 20 turns on each and every
> spot of the wall, but there seems to be no secret door.
There is, but sometimes they are hard to find, and sometimes they are really
hard to find. Keep searching, eventualy you will find it or starve to death.
It's also possible that you missed something higher in the dungeon.


> Thanks folks!

One more thing is  the "/" command. When you hit the / key you get the
option to identify object with cursor. If you pick yes you move the cursor
object you want information on otherwise you type in the name. What this
does is give information on stuff in the game. Most of the information is in
the form of various quotes from literature. For example if you want
information on grid bugs it will come up with a quote from Tron with author
and title credited. You can use this on most stuff in the game including
most gods, and other nifty stuff.

This information isn't really a spoiler. For the most part it's flavor text,
but it can be useful so you know what you are fighting.

Good luck.



